{"kind":"report","text":"API PARITY REVIEW across the five surfaces, at main d91fb8b6. Judgement, not a matrix.\n\nTHE FRAME THAT MADE IT TRACTABLE. These are not five implementations of one API; they are three layers wearing five coats, and the layering is mostly healthy. Bounded queries - work, artifacts, inspect - all call the same internal/statusview builders on CLI, MCP and resident, so a selector means one thing everywhere. That is the pattern. The defects are where something does not follow it. host/ is deliberately a different contract with four acts and no vocabulary, has no parity obligation, and showed no drift.\n\nTHE HIGHEST-COST FINDING, AND I CONFIRMED IT MYSELF. The MCP has NO review tool. The only occurrences of the word in cmd/gitseq-mcp are diagnostic strings inside projectionNotes. Meanwhile SKILL.md - a document titled how to work in the gitseq workroom over its MCP server - says at line 155: File a verdict with gs review. So the surface agents actually use mandates a helper it does not have. An agent files a verdict by hand-building body.verdict, head and artifact plus an assembled rests_on, with none of the validation gs review performs: verdict enum, checkout-at-head, promise ownership, reviewer independence, artifact co-signing for multi-path retirement. The MCP projectionNotes that diagnose after the fact that body.verdict is set but the fold projected no review are an admission this bites, and the log is append-only so each miss is permanent.\n\nI watched this happen tonight. ox-checker hand-filed its custody verdict, said so in the filing note, and its earlier promise on that chain was ruled ineffective. That is not a hypothetical cost; it is the review I read an hour ago. Fix: extract validateReview and the verdict assembly from cmd/gs into an internal package and expose an MCP review tool over it. That is also a simplification - cmd/gs/main.go is the largest command file in the tree.\n\nSECOND: THE CLI IGNORES THE RESIDENT THE REPOSITORY ADVERTISES. internal/app/resident.go publishes the URL into the repo; the MCP auto-discovers it on every call; the CLI requires --server typed every time and otherwise does a full local verified fold, including for writes. The safety argument for auto-discovery already exists inside the CLI: askResident refuses any resident answer whose frontier is not the checkout head. Every operator here has independently learned the --server discipline as a workaround. Default it from ResidentURL with the existing frontier validation, and keep an explicit way to force local.\n\nTHIRD, AND CHEAP TO FIX BEFORE IT BITES. Error contracts are prose matched by string equality across a process boundary. The resident answers failures as 400 with a prose error; the MCP then branches on refusal.Message == \"credential is not valid\" in three places, which I verified at cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:1231, :1345 and :1372 against internal/service/server.go:628 and ui.go:169. Reword one refusal and session repair breaks silently. A machine code field, or honest status codes, turns three string comparisons into one field check.\n\nON THE VERDICT SHAPE, WHICH THE REQUEST ASKED ABOUT DIRECTLY. The readers have already converged: the fold projects a review only from body.verdict and the merge gate reads body.verdict, head and artifact. Nothing authoritative reads body.status or bare text any more. What remains is that the WRITE side is unconstrained everywhere except gs review, so wrong-shaped verdicts still land and merely fail to project - which is exactly the incident class we keep hitting - and that statusview must read a reported head as body[\"head\"] falling back to body[\"commit\"], with body[\"status\"] surfaced as report_status. I verified that fallback at internal/statusview/query.go:367-371. The single readable shape to declare: a review report is body.verdict in {approved, changes-requested} plus body.head plus body.artifact; commit belongs to artifacts; status is either declared for closure reports or retired.\n\nTWO OF THE BRIEF EXAMPLES HAVE EXPIRED and I am recording that rather than repeating them. gs inspect and MCP inspect both take exactly one event now. Verdict readers are already unified. The live drift at this head is concentrated on the write side and the error contract, not the query side, which the shared statusview layer largely fixed.\n\nSMALLER, RANKED BELOW THOSE. The UI can utter what it cannot make well-formed: its composer writes only mentions as body, so a UI report can never carry a verdict and a request cannot be filed at all, while /v0/act accepts arbitrary body and kind. Either declare the UI read-mostly or give the composer the fields. /v0/act also returns only the record, dropping the replay flag, the kind warning and every projection note, so the browser write path has none of the feedback the MCP path has. #N is minted by the UI from decision.sequence and resolves nowhere else, so a human quoting a ticket number hands every other caller a handle nothing accepts. And POST /v0/status has no caller but its own tests, while /v0/graph is already documented as dead.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT RECOMMENDING. Unifying status. The three shapes are audience-fit, share one builder, and each labels its source. The name collision is the only cost and renaming would cost more.\n\nNOT FILING CHILD REQUESTS YET, for the same reason as the injection review: every actor is loaded and ox-checker queue holds only the item I already deferred. Two findings now warrant requests - the MCP review tool and the resident default - and I will route them when a builder frees rather than restock a queue I complained about an hour ago.","body":{"commit":"d91fb8b633d78b224c388eb96098825d9fd42c4b"}}